meta {
  name: Download during S3 outage (manual)
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/download/:fileName?expires={{lastExpires}}&linkId={{lastLinkId}}&signature={{lastSignature}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  expires: {{lastExpires}}
  linkId: {{lastLinkId}}
  signature: {{lastSignature}}
}

params:path {
  fileName: {{lastFileName}}
}

headers {
  Accept: application/octet-stream
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

tests {
  test("returns 503 when the object store is unreachable", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(503);
    expect(res.getBody().errorCode).to.equal('STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE');
    expect(res.getHeader('retry-after')).to.equal('30');
  });
}

docs {
  MANUAL RUNBOOK - not safe to run unattended in CI. Only the S3-outage-during-download path is currently proven
  by mocked unit tests (DownloadServiceTest), never against the real docker-compose stack. Steps:

  1. `docker compose up -d` the full stack and run Upload -> Get signed download link to mint a FRESH link
     immediately before step 2 - do not reuse a link any sibling request in this folder has already touched (the
     server's 3-max-redemption count is shared and does not reset between requests; running the whole folder
     back-to-back will otherwise get a false 404 LINK_EXPIRED here instead of the 503 this request is meant to
     prove, as verified against the running service).
  2. `docker compose stop floci` (the S3-compatible object store container) while the link is still within its
     3-minute expiry window.
  3. Send this request - expect 503 STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE with Retry-After: 30.
  4. `docker compose start floci` to restore the stack afterwards.
}
